<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Taxila</h1><p class="st-meta">350 BCE – 317 BCE (approx.) · capital Taxila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of King Ambhi, who welcomed Alexander the Great to Taxila in 326 BCE and joined him against his rival Porus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Achaemenid control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> region faded in the 4th century BCE, a local kingdom emerged around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, the old capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Kingdom of Gandhara</a>, between the Indus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>. Its ruler, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a> and in Indian tradition as Ambhi, was at odds with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paurava-kingdom">Paurava kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> was a wealthy city on the trade route from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Bactria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and a famous centre of learning; tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, taught there. Greek writers described its customs, including the exposure of the dead to vultures and the sale of girls in marriage markets. When Alexander arrived in 326 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Ambhi</a> submitted with gifts and provided troops and elephants for the campaign.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Alexander confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Ambhi</a> as ruler under Macedonian oversight. After Alexander's death the Greek commanders left the region, and around 317 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> was absorbed by Chandragupta's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, becoming the seat of its north-western province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 350 BCE – 317 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:52.74%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 BCE – 317 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taxila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ambhi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taxila (Ambhi), Takshashila</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-334&amp;focus=kingdom-of-taxila" aria-label="Kingdom of Taxila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6538 954 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Taxila lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6858 1210L5548 987M6858 1210L7253 1492"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5548 987h0M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6858 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6858 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Taxila. <a href="/atlas?year=-334&amp;focus=kingdom-of-taxila">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

